    id: "4a21a13d1f69eb5d6cbc",
    cat: "geopolitica",
    title: "Singapore launches matchmaking service for government workers",
    summary: "The launch of FirstDate comes as the ageing nation wrestles with a record-low fertility rate.",
    preview: "Singapore has rolled out a matchmaking service for its government workers, an initiative that comes as the country wrestles with a record-low fertility rate. FirstDate, which launched this week for those aged 21 to 35, boasts \"a different approach to dating\", where it uses a Nobel-winning algorithm to match participants to one date at a time. \"Swipe fatigue ends here,\" its website reads. Participants sign in using the national digital identity system and answer a survey about their interests, habits and values.",
    body: "Singapore has rolled out a matchmaking service for its government workers, an initiative that comes as the country wrestles with a record-low fertility rate.\n\nFirstDate, which launched this week for those aged 21 to 35, boasts \"a different approach to dating\", where it uses a Nobel-winning algorithm to match participants to one date at a time. \"Swipe fatigue ends here,\" its website reads.\n\nParticipants sign in using the national digital identity system and answer a survey about their interests, habits and values.\n\nFirstDate's launch drew mixed responses among Singaporeans, with some describing it as \"dystopian\" and others saying they would be keen to give it a shot.\n\nOn its website, FirstDate highlights the Gale-Shapley Stable Marriage algorithm which powers the programme.\n\nThe mathematical framework pairs two equally sized groups of people based on their preferences.\n\nThe late mathematician Lloyd Shapley was awarded the 2012 Nobel memorial prize in economic sciences for discovering the algorithm, which has been used in public school admissions, medical residency matching, and dating apps, among other things.\n\n\"At the end of the day, I think the experience of meeting someone through an application doesn't change much across the [different types of] applications,\" Elizabeth, a 29-year-old who works in Singapore's defence ministry and is keen on trying FirstDate out told the BBC.\n\n\"But it is nice to know that the likelihood of being scammed is much lower. We're all public servants looking for a partner, right?\"\n\nBut some social media users say the initiative, which is currently in its pilot phase, discriminates against those not working in government.\n\nOthers criticised the age limit. \"They are only doing this to raise the total fertility rate. They don't actually [care] about whether we find a partner otherwise.\n\n\"Since there are higher risks involved in having kids at 35 and older, the government just writes [the older singles] off,\" wrote another Reddit user.\n\nIt is not clear if the pilot programme will be available to the wider public in future. The BBC has reached out to GovTech, the statutory board behind the pilot, for comment.\n\nThe pilot launch comes as Singapore's birth rate has slumped to historic lows. For the first time in the past 60 years, fewer than 30,000 babies were born in 2025.\n\nThe number of deaths meanwhile have been rising steadily in the greying nation, hitting about 26,500 in 2025.\n\nIn August, Prime Minister Lawrence Wong announced a series of new measures to better support families and to encourage couples to have children, including almost S$70,000 ($54,000; £41,000) in financial support for every child by the time they turn 17.\n\nFirstDate is not the first time the citystate is trying to play Cupid to its citizens.\n\nIn 1984, it set up the Social Development Unit (SDU) tasked with organising social events to encourage graduate singles to mingle.\n\nIts exclusive target audience of university graduates was criticised as being elitist, but authorities justified the approach saying non-graduates did not seem to have difficulty finding partners. The SDU was eventually phased out.\n\nThe next year, the government set up another agency to promote marriages among non-graduates.\n\nThe idea of a government-run dating service is not unique to Singapore. In September 2024, Tokyo's metropolitan government launched an AI-powered dating app in an effort to combat the country's declining birth rate.\n\nThe app, which is open to single residents in the city, resulted in 265 couples getting married, according to government data released in July this year.\n\nAdditional",
